---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
import Link from "../../components/Link.astro"
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro"
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import quadratic from "../../img/p5/quadratic.png"
import cubic from "../../img/p5/cubic.png"
import live_server from "../../img/p5/live-server.png"
import complex_plane from "../../img/p5/complex-plane-3-4i.svg"
import sin_cos_animation from "../../img/sin-cos-animation.gif"
---
<Slide t2="Setup (P5.js, VS Code)">
    <ol class="text-sm-1">
        <li>Log into P5.js site</li>
        <li>Fetch an empty P5.js project (can download from the web site)</li>
        <li>Open the folder (or an enclosing folder) in VS Code</li>
        <li>"Go Live" in VS Code&mdash;needs Live Server package</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ live_server }
            alt="VS Code Live Server"
            height="500"
            width="500" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Math.js">
    <ul>
        <li>We can't use the NPM package manager - P5.js sketches are
            structured differently</li>
        <li>Download <code>math.js</code> from:
            <Link href="https://mathjs.org/download.html" /></li>
        <li>Place in folder alongside <code>index.html</code></li>
        <li>Edit <code>index.html</code></li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Loading Math.js">
    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="html" height="400px" lines="*|6">
            { `
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <script src="https://[...]/p5.js"></script>
    <script src="https://[...]/p5.sound.min.js"></script>
    <script src=${"math.js"}></script>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
</head>

<body>
    <main>
    </main>
    <script src=${"sketch.js"}></script>
</body>
</html>
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="First Sketch">
    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="400px" lines="*|3-4,10-11">
            { `
function setup() {
    createCanvas(400, 400)
    textSize(36)
    textAlign(CENTER)
}

function draw() {
    background(220)

    const i = math.sqrt(9)
    text(i, width / 2, height / 2)
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Onward!" visible={ false }>
    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ quadratic }
            alt="Quadratic Plot"
            height="300"
            width="300" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Fundamentals: Math.js">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">The library we are using (Math.js) is referred to in the code as
            <code>math</code>; the built-in
            JavaScript library is called <code>Math</code></li>
        <li class="fragment">The two libraries have a lot of the same functions</li>
        <li class="fragment">Math.js can deal with complex numbers like
            <span class="text-yellow">\( \sqrt{-1} \)</span> which <code>Math</code> cannot</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="The Complex Plane">
    <ul>
        <li>Complex numbers have a <em>real</em> and an <em>imaginary</em> part</li>
        <li>Either of these parts can be 0</li>
        <li>If the imaginary part is 0, the number is a conventional real number</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ complex_plane }
            alt="The Complex Plane"
            height="300"
            width="300" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Evaluation">
    <ul class="text-sm-2">
        <li class="fragment">Math.js can also evaluate mathematical equations</li>
        <li class="fragment">(We can try this in <code>node</code>: new folder,
            do a <code>npm install mathjs</code> first)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="100px">
            { `
math.evaluate("x^3 - x/2", {x : 10})  // -> 995
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <ul class="text-sm-2">
        <li class="fragment">(JavaScript can obviously evaluate expressions as well, but is not as
            powerful&mdash;it can't process complex numbers, for instance)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="100px">
            { `
math.evaluate("sqrt(-1)")  // "Complex { re: 0, im: 1 }", in other words i
// Compare with: Math.sqrt(-1)
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Simple Evaluator Sketch">
    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="375px">
            { `
const coordinates = math.range(-width/2, width/2).toArray()

for (let xPixel of coordinates) {
    // Convert pixels to -1.0..1.0 range:
    let x = map(xPixel, -width/2, width/2, -1, 1)

    /* Evaluate a maths expression, passing
        in a value for "x": */
    let y = math.evaluate("x^3 - x/2", {x : x})

    // Convert -1.0..1.0 back to pixels:
    let yPixel = map(y, -1, 1, height/2, -height/2)
    circle(xPixel, yPixel, 10)
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Result">
    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ cubic }
            alt="Cubic Plot"
            height="400"
            width="400" />
    </div>

</Slide>

<Slide t2="Evaluator: Key Notes">
    <ul>
        <li>We are doing all of our graph plotting in x and y coordinates
            between -1 and 1</li>
        <li>We have to map the x pixel positions of our dots across the canvas
            into this range</li>
        <li>We have to map the result of the equation calculation back into pixels</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Mathematical Notes">
    <ul>
        <li>A mathematical equation has as many solutions as its
            <em>degree</em> (linear, quadratic, cubic and so on)
        </li>
        <li>
            Some or all of the roots (solutions) might be complex numbers
            (e.g. the two solutions for <span class="text-yellow">\( x^2 = -1 \)</span>)
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Trigonometry">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">We can move away from simple polynomials (\( x^2 \), \( x^3 \) etc.)</li>
        <li class="fragment">The most common trigonometric functions: sine (<code>sin</code>)
            and cosine (<code>cos</code>)</li>
        <li class="fragment">Let's try those in P5</li>
        <li class="fragment">Bonus: combinations of sine and cosine
            at different frequencies
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Sine and Cosine (Animated)">
    <div class="fragment flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ sin_cos_animation }
            alt="Sine/Cosine animation"
            height="500"
            width="500" />
    </div>
</Slide>